OBJECTS

An object is a part of a program that stores information and can perform actions.

Programs use objects to complete different tasks. C# is an object-oriented programming language, which means it uses objects as important parts of a program.

Objects have two main parts:

* Properties – information or data that belongs to an object.
* Methods – actions that an object can perform.

CONTROLS

Controls are objects that users can see and use in a program’s GUI (Graphical User Interface).

Some common controls are:

* Label
* Button
* TextBox

Controls make programs easier and more useful.

Some objects are not visible on the GUI. Examples include:

* Timer
* OpenFileDialog

A class is a piece of code that defines the structure and behavior of a type of object.

GETTING STARTED WITH VISUAL STUDIO

Visual Studio is a professional IDE (Integrated Development Environment) used to create and develop programs.

The main parts of Visual Studio include:

* Designer Window
* Solution Explorer
* Properties Window
* Toolbox

TOOLBOX

The Toolbox is used to choose controls that you want to add to your application.

It is usually located on the left side of Visual Studio.

The Toolbox contains different groups, such as:

* All Windows Forms
* Common Controls

TOOLTIPS

A Tooltip is a small information box that appears when you place the mouse pointer over an item in Visual Studio.

It helps you understand what a button or tool does.

SPECIFYING THE PROJECT NAME

When you save a new project for the first time, you can choose the project name.

The project name helps you identify your application.

DISPLAYING THE DESIGNER

Sometimes a form does not automatically appear in the Designer when you open an existing project.

To open it:

1. Right-click Form1.cs in Solution Explorer.
2. Select View Designer.

The form will then appear in the Designer.

A form in the Designer has a bounding box around it. The box has small handles that allow you to resize the form.

ADDING CONTROLS TO A FORM

You can add controls from the Toolbox to your form.

For example, to add a Button:

* Double-click the Button control, or
* Drag the Button from the Toolbox onto the form.

After adding a control, you can:

* Resize it.
* Move it.
* Change its properties.

DELETING A CONTROL

To delete a control:

1. Select the control.
2. Press the Delete key on the keyboard.

RULES FOR NAMING CONTROLS

Controls have names that are used to identify them in C# code.

The main naming rules are:

* The first character must be a letter or an underscore _.
* The other characters can be letters, numbers, or underscores.
* Spaces are not allowed in names.

